---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp-lua/img/bp-pong-target.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const HeldKeys = `if love.keyboard.isDown("w") then
    leftY = leftY - 300 * dt
end`;

const DrawScore = `love.graphics.print(leftScore .. "   " .. rightScore, 380, 30)`;

const BounceTop = `if ballY < 10 then
    ballDY = -ballDY
end`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Bonus Project: Pong</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">A real two-player game, on one keyboard.</b>{" "}
            Two paddles, a ball that speeds around the screen, and a score that
            goes up. It is the oldest video game worth building and it is still
            fun to play against someone sitting next to you.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This is extra credit and it is optional.</b> Nothing later in the
            course depends on it. It exists because you finished early, or
            because you want to build something you can show somebody.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="before">
          <h2>Before You Start</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">You need everything through session 14.</b>{" "}
            Specifically: variables, <CodeSnippet code="love.update(dt)" lang="lua" inline />,{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="if" lang="lua" inline /> statements, keyboard
            input, and collision as a condition. If you have not done session 14
            yet, come back &mdash; you would be guessing, and that is not what
            this is for.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>One thing here is new.</b> Drawing text on the screen:
          </p>

          <Code code={DrawScore} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet code="love.graphics.print" lang="lua" inline /> takes
            the text, then an x and a y. That is the whole thing. It is the same{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code=".." lang="lua" inline /> you already use to join
            text together.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Copy <CodeSnippet code="template-game" lang="txt" inline /> and
            rename it <CodeSnippet code="bp-pong" lang="txt" inline />. No new
            files, no libraries, nothing to install.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="tasks">
          <h2>Build It</h2>

          <p>
            Five stages. <b class="text-sn-theme">Run it after every one.</b> A
            stage that works is worth more than three stages you cannot test.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">1. Two paddles that move</h3>

          <p>
            Draw two tall thin rectangles, one near each side. Give each one a
            variable for its y position. Then move them: <b>W</b> and <b>S</b>{" "}
            for the left paddle, <b>Up</b> and <b>Down</b> for the right.
          </p>

          <p>
            You need a kind of key check you have not used yet.{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="love.keypressed" lang="lua" inline /> fires once
            when a key goes down, which is wrong here &mdash; a paddle should
            keep moving while the key is held:
          </p>

          <Code code={HeldKeys} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            That goes in <CodeSnippet code="love.update" lang="lua" inline />,
            and the <CodeSnippet code="dt" lang="lua" inline /> is what makes the
            paddle move at the same speed on every computer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">2. A ball that moves on its own</h3>

          <p>
            One variable for the ball's x, one for its y, and{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">one for each direction it is traveling in</b>{" "}
            &mdash; call them something like{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="ballDX" lang="lua" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="ballDY" lang="lua" inline />. Every frame, add
            each direction to its position.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">3. Bounce off the top and bottom</h3>

          <p>
            When the ball reaches an edge, flip the direction it is moving
            vertically:
          </p>

          <Code code={BounceTop} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            <b>That minus sign is the whole trick.</b> Going down becomes going
            up. Do the same at the bottom of the screen.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">4. Bounce off the paddles</h3>

          <p>
            This is the hard one, and it is the reason this project comes after
            session 14. The ball bounces off the left paddle when{" "}
            <b>two things are true at once</b>: it has reached the paddle's x,{" "}
            <i>and</i> its y is between the top and bottom of that paddle. Flip{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="ballDX" lang="lua" inline /> when both hold.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Get this wrong in the easy way and the ball bounces off the whole
            left wall. That is worth seeing once before you fix it &mdash; it
            tells you exactly which half of the condition you are missing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">5. Score</h3>

          <p>
            If the ball goes off the left side, the right player scores. Add one
            to their score, put the ball back in the middle, and keep playing.
            Draw both scores at the top with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="love.graphics.print" lang="lua" inline />.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>What Success Looks Like</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image
              src={TargetImage}
              alt="A dark 800 by 600 window with a white paddle at the left edge, a white paddle at the right edge, a white ball below center, and the score zero zero at the top"
              width={800}
              class="img-fluid rounded"
            />
          </div>

          <p>
            Both paddles move while their keys are held. The ball bounces off
            the top, the bottom, and both paddles, and{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">the rally can go on as long as both
            players keep up.</b> When someone misses, the score changes and the
            ball restarts in the middle.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="extend">
          <h2>Then Make It Yours</h2>

          <ul>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Stop the paddles leaving the screen.</b> Right now they slide
              straight off the top if you hold the key. One condition each.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Speed the ball up on every hit.</b> A rally that gets harder is
              a much better game than one that does not.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Fix the sticky paddle.</b> Hit the very edge of a paddle and the
              ball can flip direction several frames in a row, so it shudders
              instead of bouncing. Work out why, then fix it. This is a real bug
              in real games, and finding it yourself is worth more than the rest
              of this list.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Play to eleven.</b> Stop the game and say who won.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Make it a one-player game.</b> Move the right paddle toward the
              ball automatically. Then make it beatable, which is harder than
              making it good.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Submit whatever you have.</b> A working rally with no score still
            earns credit, and so does a finished game with a bug you can
            describe.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Project Complete
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
